  1. (1)

    Risk problems that affect different urban constituencies at the same time can be managed in accordance with each unique set of spatial conditions specificity;

  2. (2)

    An inherent degree of overlap and redundancy makes nonhierarchical adaptive and integrative risk governance systems more resilient and therefore less vulnerable (Renn and Klinke 2014);

  3. (3)

    The larger number of actors facilitates experimentation and learning (Renn 2008);

  4. (4)

    This approach includes the experiences from direct community-building efforts and links the various agency levels into an integrated structure of governance (Higo et al. 2017; Okada 2018);

  5. (5)

    This approach is sensitive to different cultural contexts and community situations (Renn and Schweizer 2009).

